---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import m from '../data/manifest.json';
import pairsConfig from '../data/pairs_meta.json';

// Only enabled pairs are listed. The table is generated from config/pairs.yaml via
// the export, so the forms and rationales cannot drift from what the study measures.
const rows = (pairsConfig as any[]).filter(p => p.enabled);
---

<Base title="About" description="Why these name pairs, and what correct transliteration carries.">
  <div class="prose-wrap">
    <h1>About this study</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      In 2018 Ukraine's Ministry of Foreign Affairs launched <strong>#CorrectUA</strong>, asking the
      world to write Ukrainian names the Ukrainian way rather than through Russian. The campaign is
      remembered for Kyiv, but the same rules govern people, dishes and historical figures. This site
      measures how far the shift reached, across {m.num_sources} sources and {m.time_span}, by asking
      one question of every record: which spelling did the author use?
    </p>

    <h2 id="why-these">Why these names</h2>

    <p>
      The sample is built around a legally codified and internationally recognised transliteration
      standard. Ukraine's Cabinet of Ministers established a unified table for rendering proper
      names in Latin script, applying it to both personal and geographical names. That system was
      submitted to the United Nations as Ukraine's national romanization standard, approved in
      2012, and adopted by BGN/PCGN in 2019.
    </p>

    <p>
      The pairs are chosen to maximise contrast at exactly the graphemic points where Ukrainian and
      Russian diverge in Latin script. The official table makes these explicit: Ukrainian
      <strong>Г</strong> becomes <em>H</em>, not <em>G</em>; <strong>И</strong> becomes <em>y</em>;
      <strong>Я</strong> becomes <em>ya</em> or <em>ia</em> by position. Letter-by-letter rendering
      produces <em>Bohdan</em>, <em>Volodymyr</em>, <em>Kyiv</em>, <em>Odesa</em>,
      <em>Feodosiia</em>. These are the precise positions where Russian-derived spellings enter
      English, so each pair tests a structurally meaningful rule rather than adding another example
      of the same one.
    </p>

    <p>
      Naming is not a single-variable phenomenon, which is why the set spans several kinds of writing. Place names sit at the intersection of orthography, cartography and
      sovereignty — the official system governs passports, maps, registers and signage, making
      toponyms the domain where linguistic form becomes public international standard. Crimean
      names such as <em>Feodosiia</em> matter because the international legal framework continues
      to affirm Ukraine's territorial integrity within its recognised borders.
    </p>

    <p>
      Historical names test something different: whether English has shifted only in current-news
      contexts or also in deeper historiographical language. Personal names are the human-scale
      equivalent of the same shift — the rules that yield <em>Bohdan</em> and <em>Mykola</em> also
      explain why Галина should appear as <em>Halyna</em> rather than the Russian-derived
      <em>Galina</em>. Food names function as heritage markers: UNESCO's inscription of Ukrainian
      borshch cooking confirms that culinary vocabulary carries symbolic ownership.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      Some entries are deliberate edge cases. <em>Cossack</em> is not a transliteration at all but
      an entrenched exonym, and marking it as such helps locate where the problem stops being
      letter-for-letter transmission and becomes imperial exonymy. Religious and heritage
      institutions are included because archive-heavy naming tends to preserve older Russian-based
      English forms far longer than news media do.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pairs">The {rows.length} pairs</h2>
    <p>
      Each row gives the Ukrainian form, its transliteration under the official standard, the
      Russian-derived form that competes with it in English, and what the choice carries.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="pairs-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Ukrainian</th>
            <th>From Ukrainian</th>
            <th>From Russian</th>
            <th>What the choice carries</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map(p => (
            <tr>
              <td class="cyr">{p.ukrainian_cyrillic || '—'}</td>
              <td><a href={`/pair/${p.slug}`}>{p.ukrainian}</a></td>
              <td class="ru">{p.russian}</td>
              <td class="sig">{p.significance || '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="resources">Official resources</h2>
    <ul class="res">
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.kmu.gov.ua/npas/243262567" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Cabinet of Ministers Resolution</a> — the transliteration table, with guidelines attached
        to the linked file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://dmsu.gov.ua/services/transliteration.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          State Migration Service transliteration tool</a> — check the correct rendering of a name.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.sapiens.org/language/ukraine-versus-the-ukraine/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Ukraine versus “the Ukraine”</a> — on the definite article and statehood.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="more">Go deeper</h2>
    <ul class="res">
      <li><a href="/sources">Sources</a> — what each of the {m.num_sources} sources covers, how far
        back it reaches, and where its blind spots are.</li>
      <li><a href="/methodology">Methodology</a> — how a record is matched and counted, and the
        limitations that follow from each source's shape.</li>
      <li><a href="/llm">AI audit</a> — which spelling large language models produce when asked
        directly, and when writing unprompted.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="open">Data and code</h2>
    <p>
      Every figure on this site is produced by a reproducible pipeline. The collection code is on
      <a href="https://github.com/IvanDobrovolsky/kyivnotkiev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
      and the full dataset is published on
      <a href="https://huggingface.co/KyivNotKiev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugging Face</a>,
      so the numbers can be checked rather than taken on trust. Methods, caveats and known
      limitations are set out in the <a href="/methodology">methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose-wrap { max-width: 100%; margin: 0; }
  /* Prose still gets a comfortable measure; the table and lists use the full
     container width like every other page. */
  .prose-wrap > p, .prose-wrap > .note { max-width: 46rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .prose-wrap p { line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .prose-wrap h2 { margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .note {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border, #e5e7eb); padding-left: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted, #6b7280);
  }

  /* The table has four columns and a prose column; on a phone it cannot compress, so
     it scrolls on its own axis rather than forcing the page to scroll sideways. */
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .pairs-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; min-width: 640px; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .pairs-table th {
    text-align: left; font-size: 0.78rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--text-muted, #6b7280); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border, #e5e7eb);
    padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .pairs-table td {
    padding: 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #f1f3f5; vertical-align: top;
  }
  .pairs-table tr:hover td { background: #fafbfc; }
  .cyr { white-space: nowrap; color: var(--text-muted, #6b7280); }
  .ru { white-space: nowrap; color: #dc2626; }
  .pairs-table td a { font-weight: 600; color: #0057B8; text-decoration: none; }
  .pairs-table td a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .sig { color: var(--text-muted, #6b7280); line-height: 1.5; min-width: 18rem; }
  .res li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; line-height: 1.6; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .lede { font-size: 1rem; }
  }
</style>
